      <section class="lf-article-section"><h2>1. Why roofing lead prices vary</h2><p>Roofing lead costs are affected by service type, location, competition, seasonality, campaign quality and the level of qualification. A residential repair inquiry and a qualified commercial replacement opportunity may have very different economics.</p><p>Quotes for shared leads, exclusive leads, Google advertising, Meta campaigns and agency-managed programs may include different services. Do not compare their prices without understanding what is actually delivered.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>2. Calculate cost per inquiry</h2><p>Cost per inquiry equals marketing spend divided by the number of inquiries generated. If a campaign spends $2,000 and produces 40 inquiries, the cost per inquiry is $50.</p><p>This is a hypothetical example, not an industry average. It includes no information about whether those people own property, need roofing work or are within your service area.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>3. Calculate cost per qualified lead</h2><p>A qualified roofing inquiry should meet your business&#x27;s agreed criteria. These may include location, project type, property ownership, scope, timeline and the ability to contact the prospect.</p><p>If the same $2,000 campaign produces only 16 qualified opportunities, the cost per qualified lead is $125. This can be more useful than the $50 raw inquiry cost.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>4. Calculate cost per booked inspection</h2><p>Not every qualified lead schedules an inspection. Some customers are comparing options, some cannot be reached, and others change their plans.</p><p>If eight inspections are booked from the $2,000 campaign, cost per booked inspection is $250. Separately track completed inspections so cancellations and no-shows do not disappear from reporting.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>5. Calculate customer acquisition cost</h2><p>Customer acquisition cost divides the cost of acquiring customers by the number of new customers acquired. Decide which marketing, agency, creative and sales costs belong in your calculation, then apply that definition consistently.</p><p>If two jobs are won from the example campaign, advertising spend alone works out to $1,000 per won job. Fully loaded acquisition cost may be higher if it includes additional marketing or sales expenses.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>6. Understand revenue versus return on ad spend</h2><p>Revenue generated from won jobs is not the same as profit. Return on ad spend or ROAS is commonly calculated as attributed revenue divided by advertising spend.</p><p>If two roofing jobs produce $24,000 in attributed revenue from $2,000 of advertising spend, ROAS is 12:1. This does not mean the contractor earned $22,000 in profit. Materials, labor, overhead and other costs still need to be considered.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>7. Evaluate actual marketing profitability</h2><p>For a useful profitability calculation, estimate the contribution from acquired jobs after relevant fulfillment costs, then subtract the costs of acquiring them. Define your cost basis clearly so figures are comparable across periods.</p><p>For example, if attributed jobs produce $8,000 in contribution before acquisition costs, and the full acquisition cost is $3,000, the remaining contribution is $5,000. Using acquisition cost as the denominator, the return is approximately 167%. This is illustrative and depends on the stated assumptions.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>8. Compare marketing channels fairly</h2><p>Google Search, Meta advertising, referrals, local SEO and third-party lead providers can produce customers at different stages of buying intent. A cheaper raw inquiry does not always produce a cheaper customer.</p><p>Compare like-for-like periods, project types and outcomes. A campaign for high-value replacements should not necessarily be judged using the same target acquisition cost as a small repair service.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>9. Connect ad platforms with CRM outcomes</h2><p>Record lead sources, booking outcomes, estimates and won revenue where practical. Maintain reliable CRM status definitions and avoid double-counting jobs that came through several touchpoints.</p><p>Google Ads supports offline conversion measurement and enhanced conversions for leads. These can help link advertising activity to qualified leads or completed sales when properly implemented and permitted.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>10. Use the calculator as a planning tool</h2><p>An estimate of potential sales is useful for scenario planning, but it is not a guaranteed forecast or a complete ROI measurement. Your assumptions about qualified lead rates, close rates and average job value matter.</p><p>The LeadsFlo Roofing Revenue Calculator helps explore potential revenue scenarios. For actual ROI, include acquisition costs and the profitability of completed roofing projects.</p></section>

      
